  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some areas of frost tonight. Otherwise, \ngenerally tranquil weather is expected to prevail through the \nmiddle portion of next week.\n\nHigh pressure will remain in place within the vicinity of our area \nthrough the middle of next week with continuing mild and dry \nconditions. \n\nTemperatures are cooler today compared to yesterday and with the \nhigh building in right over our area for tonight, this will lead to \nvery good radiational cooling conditions. Temperatures should fall \nquickly during the evening into the upper 30s and low 40s as winds \ndecouple with the high pressure center nearby, then continue slowly \nfalling overnight. A Frost Advisory remains in effect for portions \nof SE PA (not including Philly and Delco) into southern NJ where the \ngrowing season remains active with overnight lows in these areas \ngenerally in the mid 30s. North of these areas generally expect lows \ngetting down into the low 30s while Delmarva and the urban corridor \nfrom around Wilmington through Philly will generally see lows in the \nupper 30s to around 40. \n\nFor Sunday, we'll have a bit more in the way of some high cloudiness \naround but the winds will remain light and temperatures will \nmoderate a few degrees compared to today. \n\nScattered to broken high cloud cover will persist into next week \ndespite surface high pressure. Temperatures are expected to increase \nback to above normal values early next week, with highs in the mid \n60s to low 70s and milder overnights (no frost/freeze potential). \nDry weather is expected to persist through the day Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain is expected to return late next week. \n\nThe stretch of dry weather is expected to come to an end late next \nweek. It appears increasingly likely that a period or periods of \nrainfall will move into the area as a storm system affects the \nregion Thursday through at least Friday. Early in the week, a cold \nfront will be moving across the central part of the country. The \nparent low moves across the Great Lakes and into Canada later in the \nweek, while a secondary low develops and moves into the southern \nstates by mid week. This low is expected to strengthen as it moves \ninto the Mid Atlantic and northeast states later in the week, \nbringing our area a likely window to end our dry streak. Forecast \ndetails remain uncertain since this is still almost a week away but \nat this point the best chances for rain appear to be late day \nThursday into the day Friday. At this time, this appears it will be \na beneficial rainfall event with no significant weather hazards \nanticipated but it will have to be watched closely as the \npossibility is there for amounts to be on the heavier side depending \non how much Atlantic moisture is pulled into the system.",
